---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
    </head>
    <body>
        <Header />

        <main id="top">
            <section class="page-hero">
                <div class="container">
                    <div class="breadcrumb">
                        <a href="../index.html">Home</a>
                        <span>/</span>
                        <span style="color:#fff;">About</span>
                    </div>
                    <h1 class="fade-up">Commercial & Flat Roofing in  <em
                            style="font-style:normal;color:#9CC0FF;"
                            >New Hampshire</em
                        >
                    </h1>
                    <p class="lede-page fade-up">
                        Standing-seam metal sheds snow before it becomes an ice
                        dam and shrugs off coastal salt air that eats through
                        lesser materials. Installed properly, it is the last
                        roof most homeowners ever buy.
                    </p>
                    <div class="hero-ctas">
                        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-blue"
                            >Get a Free Estimate</a
                        >
                        <a href="tel:+16035027080" class="btn btn-ghost-w"
                            >Call (603) 502-7080</a
                        >
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
          <!-- ============ INTRO ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">What We Do</span>
      <h2>Low-Slope Roofing Built Around Your Operations</h2>
      <p class="lead">A commercial roof is an operating asset, not just a building component. When it fails you are
        dealing with tenant complaints, damaged inventory, and a capital expense you did not plan for this quarter.</p>
      <p>Vincenzo's Roofing installs, repairs, restores, and maintains low-slope and flat roofing systems for commercial
        properties across New Hampshire. We work with owner-operators, property managers, and investors — from a single
        storefront on the Seacoast to multi-building portfolios inland.</p>
      <p>The practical difference on our jobs is scheduling. We section the roof so only part is ever open, stage
        material to keep entrances and parking clear, and work evenings or weekends where the building allows. Most of
        our commercial clients stay fully operational throughout.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ ROOFING SYSTEMS ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:38px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Systems We Install</span>
        <h2>Choosing the Right Membrane</h2>
        <p class="lead">Three systems cover almost every commercial roof in New Hampshire. The right one depends on your
          building's cooling load, foot traffic, budget horizon, and what is up there already.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sys">
        <div class="s">
          <div class="top">
            <span class="tag">Most popular</span>
            <h3>TPO</h3>
            <p>White thermoplastic membrane with heat-welded seams</p>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <ul>
              <li>Reflects sunlight and lowers summer cooling costs</li>
              <li>Heat-welded seams — no adhesive joints to fail</li>
              <li>Strong resistance to punctures and chemical exposure</li>
              <li>Best value for buildings with heavy air conditioning demand</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="foot"><b>Service life:</b> 20–30 years</div>
        </div>
        <div class="s">
          <div class="top">
            <span class="tag">Best value</span>
            <h3>EPDM</h3>
            <p>Black synthetic rubber membrane, fully adhered or ballasted</p>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <ul>
              <li>Handles New Hampshire freeze-thaw cycles exceptionally well</li>
              <li>Lower installed cost than TPO on most buildings</li>
              <li>Simple to repair and patch over its service life</li>
              <li>Dark surface helps shed snow load in winter</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="foot"><b>Service life:</b> 20–30 years</div>
        </div>
        <div class="s">
          <div class="top">
            <span class="tag">Heavy duty</span>
            <h3>Modified bitumen</h3>
            <p>Multi-ply asphalt system, torch or cold-adhesive applied</p>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <ul>
              <li>Multiple layers give redundancy against punctures</li>
              <li>Handles rooftop foot traffic and service access well</li>
              <li>Good choice where equipment is regularly serviced</li>
              <li>Proven performance on older existing substrates</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="foot"><b>Service life:</b> 15–25 years</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card pale" style="margin-top:22px">
        <h3>Not sure which applies to your building?</h3>
        <p>That is what the site survey is for. We measure, check what is already installed, core-sample if the
          insulation may be wet, and give you a written recommendation with per-square-foot pricing for each viable
          option — not one number and a signature line.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PROPERTY TYPES ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Who We Work With</span>
        <h2>Properties We Roof</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="props">
        <div class="prop"><span class="n">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Retail &amp; storefronts</h3>
            <p>Strip plazas, standalone stores, and restaurants where closing for a day is not an option.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prop"><span class="n">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Office buildings</h3>
            <p>Single and multi-tenant offices, with work staged outside core business hours.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prop"><span class="n">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Multi-unit residential</h3>
            <p>Apartment buildings and condo associations, including phased work across multiple structures.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prop"><span class="n">04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Industrial &amp; warehouse</h3>
            <p>Large-span roofs with rooftop mechanical equipment and heavy drainage requirements.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prop"><span class="n">05</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Medical &amp; professional</h3>
            <p>Clinics and practices where noise, dust, and access disruption need careful management.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prop"><span class="n">06</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Municipal &amp; institutional</h3>
            <p>Public buildings and facilities, with documentation suited to procurement requirements.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ WARNING SIGNS ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Assessment</span>
        <h2>Signs Your Flat Roof Needs Attention</h2>
        <p class="lead">Flat roofs rarely fail suddenly. They give warning for months, usually somewhere nobody is
          looking.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="warn">
        <div class="w"><span class="n">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Water ponding after rain</h3>
            <p>Standing water more than 48 hours after rainfall accelerates membrane breakdown and adds serious weight
              to the structure.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="w"><span class="n">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Seams lifting or separating</h3>
            <p>Open seams are the most common entry point on any membrane roof, and the easiest failure to catch early.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="w"><span class="n">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Blisters and bubbling</h3>
            <p>Trapped moisture or air beneath the membrane. Left alone, blisters rupture and open a direct path for
              water.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="w"><span class="n">04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Interior ceiling stains</h3>
            <p>By the time a tenant reports a stain, water has usually been travelling through the insulation for some
              time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="w"><span class="n">05</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Failing flashing at penetrations</h3>
            <p>HVAC curbs, vents, and drains are where most commercial leaks begin — anywhere the membrane has been cut.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="w"><span class="n">06</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Rising energy costs</h3>
            <p>Wet insulation loses most of its R-value. An unexplained jump in heating or cooling spend often starts at
              the roof.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SCOPE ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Scope of Work</span>
      <h2>What's Included in a Commercial Re-Roof</h2>
      <p>Every commercial project we quote includes the following as standard. Where a competitor's per-square-foot
        number comes in lower, this list is usually where the difference sits.</p>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>Full site survey with measurements and photo documentation</li>
        <li>Core sampling to check for wet insulation below the membrane</li>
        <li>Tear-off and disposal of failed existing layers</li>
        <li>Deck inspection and repair where substrate has deteriorated</li>
        <li>New insulation to current R-value requirements</li>
        <li>Tapered insulation to correct drainage and eliminate ponding</li>
        <li>Membrane installed to manufacturer specification for warranty</li>
        <li>New flashing at all curbs, penetrations, and parapet walls</li>
        <li>Drain, scupper, and gutter integration checked and cleared</li>
        <li>Daily site cleanup with access and parking kept clear</li>
        <li>Manufacturer system warranty plus our workmanship warranty</li>
        <li>Close-out documentation for your records and insurers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ COST ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cost-band">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="color:#9CC0FF">Straight Pricing</span>
        <h2>How Much Does Commercial Roofing Cost in New Hampshire?</h2>
        <div class="cost-figure">$6 – $14 per sq ft</div>
        <p>Commercial roofing is quoted per square foot rather than as a flat job price. EPDM sits at the lower end, TPO
          in the middle, and fully adhered systems with new insulation and tapered drainage at the upper end. You get a
          written per-square-foot breakdown after the site survey, with each option priced separately.</p>
        <ul class="cost-drivers">
          <li><b>Membrane system —</b> EPDM, TPO, or modified bitumen</li>
          <li><b>Existing layers —</b> tear-off versus overlay where code permits</li>
          <li><b>Insulation —</b> new R-value and whether tapered drainage is needed</li>
          <li><b>Penetrations —</b> HVAC curbs, vents, and skylights all add flashing work</li>
          <li><b>Roof access —</b> crane or hoist requirements on taller buildings</li>
          <li><b>Scheduling —</b> after-hours or phased work to keep you operating</li>
        </ul>
        <div style="margin-top:30px"><a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-blue"
            style="background:#fff;color:var(--blue);box-shadow:none">Request a Site Survey</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PROCESS ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">How It Works</span>
        <h2>Your Commercial Project, Step by Step</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 01</span>
          <h3>Site survey</h3>
          <p>We measure the roof, inspect existing conditions, core-sample for wet insulation, and photograph
            everything.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 02</span>
          <h3>Written proposal</h3>
          <p>Per-square-foot pricing for each viable system, with scope, timeline, and warranty terms in writing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 03</span>
          <h3>Schedule &amp; notify</h3>
          <p>We agree phasing around your hours, pull permits, and give you notice to circulate to tenants and staff.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 04</span>
          <h3>Phased install</h3>
          <p>Only part of the roof is open at a time. Access stays clear and the building stays watertight overnight.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 05</span>
          <h3>Close-out</h3>
          <p>Final walkthrough, warranty registration, and a documentation pack for your records and insurers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ MAINTENANCE PROGRAM ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="maint">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="color:#BFD6FA">Ongoing Care</span>
        <h2>Commercial Roof Maintenance Programs</h2>
        <p>The cheapest commercial roof is the one that reaches its full service life. Most manufacturer warranties also
          require documented maintenance to stay valid — a detail that catches out a lot of building owners at claim
          time.</p>
        <div class="maint-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>Twice-yearly inspections</h3>
            <p>Spring and autumn visits, plus call-outs after major storm events.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Drain &amp; scupper clearing</h3>
            <p>The single highest-value maintenance task on any flat roof.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Seam &amp; flashing checks</h3>
            <p>Small separations sealed before they become interior leaks.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Written condition reports</h3>
            <p>Photographed findings you can file for warranty and budgeting.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ NH WEATHER ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Local Expertise</span>
        <h2>What New Hampshire Does to a Flat Roof</h2>
        <p class="lead">Low-slope roofs face problems here that simply do not exist in milder climates. Specifying for
          them is the difference between twenty years and twelve.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Snow load and drift</h3>
          <p>Flat roofs hold snow rather than shedding it, and drifting piles it against parapets and mechanical units.
            Structural capacity and drainage both have to account for a heavy February, not an average one.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Freeze-thaw on seams</h3>
          <p>Repeated expansion and contraction works at every seam and flashing joint. Heat-welded TPO seams and
            properly detailed EPDM terminations hold up far better than adhesive-only assemblies.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Ponding and spring melt</h3>
          <p>A fast thaw dumps weeks of accumulated snow into the drainage system at once. Undersized or blocked drains
            turn that into standing water — which is how most flat roof failures actually begin.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Coastal salt exposure</h3>
          <p>On Seacoast properties, salt corrodes fasteners, edge metal, and rooftop equipment supports years ahead of
            schedule. We specify corrosion-resistant components on coastal commercial work as standard.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ RECENT WORK ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
