  <!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
        <h2>Commercial Roofing Questions</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item open">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">How much does a commercial flat roof cost in New Hampshire?<span
              class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Commercial roofing in New Hampshire is generally quoted per square foot, typically between $6 and $14
            installed. EPDM sits at the lower end, TPO in the middle, and fully adhered systems with new insulation and
            tapered drainage at the upper end. Tear-off of existing layers, roof access, and rooftop equipment all
            affect the final number. We provide a written per-square-foot breakdown after a site survey.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Will my business have to close during the work?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>In almost all cases, no. We stage commercial work around your operating hours, section the roof so only
            part is open at a time, and can work evenings or weekends where the building allows. Tenants and staff are
            notified in advance, and we keep entrances and parking access clear throughout.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">What is the difference between TPO and EPDM?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>EPDM is a black rubber membrane that is durable, cost-effective, and handles New Hampshire freeze-thaw
            cycles well. TPO is a white thermoplastic membrane with heat-welded seams that reflects sunlight and lowers
            summer cooling loads. TPO usually costs slightly more up front. For buildings with significant air
            conditioning demand, TPO often pays that back.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Can a flat roof be restored instead of replaced?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Often, yes. If the membrane is intact and the insulation underneath is dry, a coating or restoration system
            can extend service life by ten to fifteen years at a fraction of replacement cost. If moisture has reached
            the insulation, restoration only traps it. We core-sample before recommending restoration so the decision is
            based on evidence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Why does water pond on my flat roof?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Flat roofs are built with a slight slope toward drains. Ponding usually means blocked drains or scuppers, a
            compressed or sagging insulation layer, or inadequate original slope. Water standing longer than 48 hours
            after rain accelerates membrane failure and adds significant weight. Tapered insulation during re-roofing
            corrects the underlying slope problem.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Do you offer maintenance programs?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Yes. We offer scheduled maintenance for property managers and building owners, typically twice a year plus
            after major storms. Each visit includes drain clearing, seam and flashing inspection, and a written
            condition report with photographs. Most manufacturer warranties require documented maintenance to stay
            valid.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SERVICE AREAS ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
